  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis relies on a single case study of a secondary city experiencing spillover from a nearby metropolis, which may limit generalizability to other secondary cities lacking similar metropolitan proximity.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the spatial expansion of Abomey-Calavi, Benin, between 1988 and 2023 using Landsat imagery classified with a supervised maximum likelihood algorithm. Built-up areas expanded tenfold from 2,213 to 23,601 hectares, primarily replacing vegetation and agricultural land which shrank by 45%. Landscape metrics and patch analysis revealed a mean annual urban growth rate of 6.11%, characterized by urban coalescence within 3 km of the city core and active diffusion beyond 6 km.",
      "why": "The findings demonstrate that urban diffusion and coalescence can occur simultaneously rather than sequentially, challenging the dominant global model of urban growth stages and providing empirical evidence from a rapidly growing Sub-Saharan secondary city."
    }
